{
  "image_name": "0c1db9ba66b28fff0d23274a5502d844.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several high-pressure, high-temperature measurement setups used to heat tiny samples and monitor their response. Across the panels, a sample is placed inside a confined cell between hard anvils or pistons, heated either electrically or by a pulsed source, and tracked with a thermocouple or optical probe.\n\nOne design uses a hot wire inside a Teflon cell and cylinder assembly, with the sample positioned a short distance from the wire while a thermocouple records temperature. Another uses an impulse-heating arrangement in a compact stack of materials such as MgO, ZrO2, Ta, and Ni, where the sample sits near a heater and thermocouple inside a small insulated chamber. The lower panels show diamond-anvil-style configurations: one combines pulsed-laser heating with detection of thermal emission from a sample next to an Ir/Pt foil, enclosed by ruby, gasket, and pressure medium; the other illustrates a probe-and-pump measurement through diamond, with a metal transducer on the sample in a pressure medium and ruby marker nearby.\n\nOverall, the image compares practical ways to deliver rapid heating and measure temperature or optical signals from microscopic samples under extreme pressure.",
